          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Why does glucose require carrier proteins to cross the cell membrane, unlike oxy</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Why does glucose require carrier proteins to cross the cell membrane, unlike oxygen which diffuses freely?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Glucose is a larger molecule that cannot pass through the hydrophobic lipid core of the bilayer</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The lipid bilayer allows small non-polar molecules like oxygen to diffuse freely, but larger molecules like glucose cannot penetrate the hydrophobic core and need carrier proteins — a process called facilitated diffusion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Glucose carries an electrical charge that is repelled by the phospholipid heads</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Glucose is an uncharged molecule. The real barrier is its size — it is too large to pass through the hydrophobic interior of the lipid bilayer without carrier proteins.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The sodium-potassium pump actively excludes glucose from the cell</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The sodium-potassium pump transports sodium and potassium ions, not glucose. Glucose crosses the membrane via carrier-mediated facilitated diffusion, which does not require ATP.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Glucose is hydrophobic and becomes trapped within the lipid bilayer</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Glucose is hydrophilic (water-soluble), not hydrophobic. Because the bilayer interior is hydrophobic, glucose cannot enter it at all and must use carrier proteins to cross.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer relates to molecular size. Small non-polar molecules like oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse freely through the hydrophobic core, but glucose is too large and requires carrier proteins for facilitated diffusion."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Selective permeability is the key principle — the bilayer freely passes small non-polar molecules but blocks larger ones like glucose, which depend on specific carrier proteins."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
